**What**
`docs/specification.adoc` is a careful normative document with 25-odd
requirement IDs, and every one of them is about `facet-git-tree`. The layer
that defines what a repository written by this project actually looks like -
ref namespaces, the kind concept, schema binding, commit-forward semantics,
locking - has no specification at all, only doc comments and a README.

**Where**
`docs/specification.adoc` (sections Compatibility, Serialization,
Deserialization, Schemas, Structural Comparison - all scoped to
`facet-git-tree`), versus `crates/gix-store/src/store.rs:14-27` where
`refs/store`, `refs/schema`, the lock directory, the lock timeout, and the CAS
retry count are all defined as private constants.

**Why it matters**
The spec explicitly hands this off twice and never picks it up. From
`facet-git-tree`s README and again as a trailing NOTE in the spec:

> Publishing a schema under a ref - e.g. `refs/schema/issue` - is a convention
> owned by higher layers such as `git-store`; `facet-git-tree` itself remains
> oid-in/oid-out and performs no ref operations.

That deferral is architecturally correct. The problem is that the higher layer
never writes it down, so the things a second implementation would need to agree
on are undocumented:

- the ref layout `<prefix>/<kind>/<name>` and `<prefix>/<kind>`
- that a data commit's schema binding is a **subtree** (`schema/`, alongside
  `value/`) inside the data commit's own tree (per `0b4a9b27`), with the
  `Schema:` trailer kept only as a human-readable provenance label naming the
  schema commit, not as the binding mechanism itself (`store.rs:275-287`
  explains why in a doc comment; nothing normative says so)
- that entity history is a first-parent walk
- what a reader should do when the `schema/` subtree is absent or its object is
  missing (`Error::SchemaObjectMissing`)
- whether a schema may be replaced incompatibly, and what that means for
  existing entities

That list is the real interoperability surface. The tree encoding is the part
another tool can reimplement from the spec today; the part it cannot
reimplement is the part that decides whether two `git-ents` tools can read each
others repositories - which matters concretely because `git-anchor`,
`git-query`, and `git-forge` are being presented alongside this one and
presumably share, or should share, the ref conventions.

Two smaller observations in the same area. `docs/` contains
`acdc-requirement.adoc`, a specification for an unrelated AsciiDoc authoring
extension, sitting beside the projects own spec with no index distinguishing
them - a reader arriving at `docs/` cannot tell which one is about `git-store`.
And the spec has no requirement covering schema evolution, which is the
question the design most invites.

**Options**
- Option A - add a `=== Storage` section to `docs/specification.adoc` with
  requirement IDs for the ref layout, the subtree schema binding, the
  provenance trailer, history semantics, and the missing-schema behaviour. Same
  style as the existing sections; the content already exists as doc comments
  and mostly needs promoting.
- Option B - a separate `docs/storage.adoc`, keeping `specification.adoc` as
  the format spec. Cleaner separation, matching the crate boundary. Trade-off:
  two documents to keep in sync, and readers have to find the second one.
- Option C - if the ref conventions are meant to be shared across the
  `git-ents` family, specify them once in a place all four projects can cite,
  rather than in any one of them.

**Recommendation**
Option A now for the ref layout and schema binding at minimum - they are the
load-bearing conventions and they are currently private constants. This is
blocked by `0b4a9b27` (Wave 1): writing the normative section before the
binding mechanism changes from trailer to subtree would just mean rewriting it
immediately after. Option C is worth considering given the family framing;
"these four tools share one ref convention, here it is" is a stronger story
than four tools that happen to look similar. High confidence that the gap
matters; medium confidence on where the text should live.
